# Handoff Mesh — Communication Layer for Multi-Agent Setups

## When to Use
- You run **more than one agent** (multi-agent strategy) and they need to hand work to each other.
- An agent finishes something and another agent (or a human) needs to pick it up.
- You want a **durable, searchable** coordination layer — not just real-time chat.
- You want to know, at a glance, what handoffs are open and whether they got done.

## What This Is
A **full-mesh handoff system** built on the vault (Obsidian) — the durable brain.
Any agent can drop a handoff into any other agent's inbox. A watcher surfaces
open handoffs hourly. Nightly maintenance archives old ones. And a
completion-reporting loop closes the deal: the agent that reads a handoff
reports back what it did before it's marked resolved.

This is the **storage + verification** half of agent communication. Real-time
channels (Buzz, Telegram) are the *talking* half; this is the *remembering and
proving* half. They complement each other — the vault stays the source of truth.

## The Layout
```
01-HANDOFFS/
    INBOX/
        <group>/                    ← one receive folder per agent/group
            <YYYY-MM-DD>-<topic>.md ← a handoff note for that agent
            _archive/               ← resolved notes (auto-moved, purged weekly)
    <from>-to-<to>/                 ← optional explicit sender→receiver folders
```

## The Protocol (4 steps — the loop)

### Step 1 — SEND: drop a handoff into the target's inbox
Write `<date>-<topic>.md` into the target group's `INBOX/<group>/` folder.
**Any agent can initiate a handoff to any peer** (Forge, Labs, Treasury,
Entertainment, HQ) — not just respond to one. The wake trigger now teaches the
send side: write the dated note into `INBOX/<target>/` with `**From:** <you>`,
`**To:** <target>`, `**Status:** open`, and a `## What's needed` section, then
commit + push. Never hand off to a peer without writing the note to their INBOX
folder — the watcher and the target's wake trigger surface it from there.
Format:
```markdown
# <topic>
**From:** <agent/group>
**To:** <group>
**Date:** <YYYY-MM-DD>
**Status:** open

## What's needed
<what the receiving agent should do>

## Context / files
<any links or paths>
```
Commit + push (or `ob sync`).

### Step 2 — WATCH: surface open handoffs automatically
Run the watcher hourly (or on each agent's wake-up). It scans every inbox lane
and reports any note still `Status: open` (not resolved, not archived).
```bash
python3 handoff-watcher.py [vault_path]
```
- **Silent when clear** — only pings when there's actually something to do.
- **Quiet overnight** — schedule it for waking hours only (e.g. 7 AM–11 PM ET).
- Wire it as a `no_agent` cron (pure script, zero tokens) so it's cheap.

### Step 3 — ACT + REPORT: the agent that reads it reports back
This is the half that makes handoffs *verified*, not just *seen*:
1. **Read** the handoff → acknowledge it in the group (never silent).
2. **Act** on it.
3. **Report back — the default return format.** When you finish, write a dated
   note to the **original sender's** return lane (`<sender>-to-<group>/`) with
   THREE sections, always:
   - **`## ✅ What was done`** — the concrete deliverable/results
   - **`## 📌 What you should know`** — blockers, wallet-control checks, caveats,
     anything that changes the picture for the receiver
   - **`## ⏭️ What's next`** — the next step, even if it's on the other agent/human
   A return note with these three sections is the default. If any is empty,
   write "None" — the structure stays. This is what lets Jordan (and any
   original sender) see the full hand-back at a glance.
4. **Mark resolved + archive** only AFTER the work is verified.

### Step 4 — CLEAN: archive weekly so it never gets too big
Nightly/weekly maintenance:
- Move resolved notes to `_archive/`.
- **Purge `_archive/` entries older than 7 days.**
- Auto-archive stale OPEN notes older than 7 days (still unresolved).

## The Watcher Script
`handoff-watcher.py` (stdlib-only, stable output — safe as a `monitor_script`):
- Lists every `<group>/` subfolder under `INBOX/`.
- Finds `*.md` notes NOT in `_archive/` and NOT marked resolved.
- Emits a short, stable report of OPEN handoffs (or nothing if all clear).

## Lifecycle states (so Jordan sees work in progress)
A handoff can carry three statuses the watcher surfaces:
- `Status: open` — just dropped, not picked up yet → watcher lists under NEW.
- `Status: in progress` (or `in route`, `picked up`, `working`, `active`,
  `awaiting review`) — an agent has ACKed and started it → watcher lists it
  under 🚧 IN PROGRESS grouped by routing direction (e.g. `gentech → labs`).
  Set this as soon as you pick up a handoff so the routing context is visible.
- `Status: resolved` / `[x]` — done + verified → watcher lists it under CLEARED.
ACK + START + REPORT is mandatory: when you pick up a handoff, (1) mark it
`Status: in progress` in a dated `<you>-to-gentech/` return note and push,
(2) start immediately, (3) report the first progress chunk or first blocker in
the same return lane. Silence after pickup is a defect — Jordan must SEE work
in progress, not wonder if an agent started.

## STALLED watchdog (closes the "is anyone working?" blind spot)
`handoff-watcher.py` also flags **STALLED** handoffs: any outbound handoff
(`gentech-to-<agent>` or `<agent>-to-<other>`) that is open, older than 6h
(by its date prefix — NOT mtime, which git re-touches), and has **no return
note** in the reverse lane. It reports each stalled handoff ONCE, then stays
silent until the agent returns or it's resolved.
- If you see a 🆘 STALLED alert: **wake the agent** (the wake-trigger re-arms
  on their next cycle) or reassign. If the stall is actually waiting on
  Jordan (funding, credentials, login), say so — the watchdog can't tell a
  stalled agent from a waiting-on-human handoff.

## STEP 3½ — AN ACK IS A VERDICT ON THE CLAIM, NOT A RECEIPT FOR THE MESSAGE

A handoff is a **claim plus a request**. Closing it by agreeing costs nothing and verifies nothing — and the
peer's premise is often stale by the time you read it, because they measured their vantage two days ago.

- **Re-measure the claim, then say what you measured.** The job still exists? The lane still 401s? The path still
  there? One command each. An answer built from the note's own text is a paraphrase of the peer's vantage, not a
  verification of yours.
- **When the finding no longer reproduces, name the action that must NOT be taken.** Measured: a peer's *"every
  authenticated call is 401"* note had healed by the time it was read — and the useful sentence was not *"it works
  now"* but **"do not rotate or reissue the key"**, because rotating a working credential destroys the ability to
  tell a future 401 from this one. **A stale finding that invites a destructive remedy is worse than silence.**
- **Split what you closed from what stays open.** A note can be answered while its root cause lives: the specific
  job was gone, and the identical defect was still present on three of the answering seat's own jobs. Report both,
  or the peer concludes the class was fixed.
- **A finding can be right and its premise wrong.** Upheld a challenge whose central sentence was correct while one
  of its cited numbers was not derivable from the source file — accept the argument, correct the arithmetic, and
  keep the two verdicts separate.
- **State the deadline and the release condition when work remains.** *"Within 7 days, or I write back with what
  blocked it"* is the difference between a closure and a promise nobody can audit.
- **Refusing to close is a valid answer.** A note that needs a human decision must never be closed by composing
  the decision yourself — see the human-identity lane rule below.

## Decision Table
| Situation | Action |
|-----------|--------|
| Agent A finishes work for Agent B | A writes handoff to B's inbox |
| Handoff sits open >1 hour | Watcher surfaces it; B picks it up |
| B reads the handoff | B ACKs (marks `in progress`), acts, reports back |
| B completes the work | B marks resolved + archives, reports what it did |
| Handoff unresolved >7 days | Nightly maintenance auto-archives it |

## ⚠️ Dispatching a WAKE to another seat — three errors that all look like the peer's fault

When you wake a peer in-process (`hermes -p <seat> chat --query-file … --oneshot`) instead of over the
mesh, three things go wrong by default. All three were measured on 2026-10-17 across a five-seat dispatch.

**1. The child INHERITS your identity — three of three seats hit this.**
`hermes -p <seat> …` does **not** scrub the parent's environment. Each child saw:

```
HERMES_SESSION_PROFILE=gentech-treasury     <-- the PARENT's identity, not its own
HERMES_HOME=/opt/data/profiles/<themselves> <-- correct
```

**A seat that reads its identity from `HERMES_SESSION_PROFILE` ACKs AS YOU.** One nearly filed a
deliverable under the wrong profile.
- **Stripping only `HERMES_SESSION_PROFILE` is not enough** — `HERMES_SESSION_KEY` still carries the
  parent (`agent:<parent>:telegram:group:…`). **Strip every `HERMES_SESSION_*` var.**
- **The trustworthy identity triple, for any seat in doubt:** `HERMES_HOME` (+ `HERMES_SCRATCH_DIR`),
  the `state.db` actually being written, and the `ps` line showing who launched it.
- Use `scripts/peer-wake.sh` if it exists — it strips every `HERMES_SESSION_*` and labels truncation.

**2. A seat's run takes 3–5 MINUTES — a short timeout truncates it mid-write.**
A 290s cap cut off three of five seats. The worst case was a seat that had **ACKed, confirmed its
count, and started the deliverable** — then died before writing it. **Cutting a seat off mid-write is
worse than not dispatching it**: you get an ACK that looks like success and no artifact.
- Default to **900s** for a real task; 600s for a light ACK request.
- **Exit 124 is a TRUNCATION, not a refusal.** Never write it up as "the seat declined."
- **Instruct the seat to write the file FIRST, then refine.** Then an interruption leaves a partial
  audit on disk instead of nothing.
- Before re-dispatching, **check the deliverable for a partial write.**

**3. "Reply in your own seat" produces ZERO shared evidence.**
The first dispatch said exactly that. Four seats read the notice, two ran 60+ tool calls, and **the
handoff file did not change by a single byte** — their findings were trapped in session logs.
**The ACK instruction must name the FILE and the line shape**, e.g. *"append one line to
`<path>` in this shape: `- **<profile> — ACK <UTC ts>.** <finding> (session <id>)`"*. Then verify by
**byte count**, not by the seat's exit code.
*Findings that live only in a session log are findings nobody can audit.*

## ⚠️ A backlog audit counts STATUSES; the work is the ASKS

When a watcher or compliance check reports a large open/uncovered count, **classify by self-declared
state before treating the number as work.** Three buckets, and only two of them need a human:

| the note says | bucket |
|---|---|
| resolved (and does **not** say `Status: open`) | **archivable** |
| `Status: open` | live — read it |
| neither marker | live — read it |

Measured on one mature lane set: **538 notes — 372 self-declared resolved, 107 blocked-and-resolved,
46 unmarked, ~9 genuinely open.** The compliance check was reporting 204 open. **Almost all of it was
closed history that had never been filed.**

**The mechanism behind the whole class: agents answer in the BODY and never move the file.** A note
reading `✅ What was done — answered / closed` under a header that still says `Status: open` is counted
open by every automated reader, forever. The work was done; the artifact lies about it.

### So resolving a handoff means MOVING the file, not editing the header

**Location is the signal every watcher agrees on.** A header edit only satisfies readers that parse
that exact marker string; a move satisfies all of them, plus every human who lists the lane.

**Filing pass, in this order:**
1. **Back up the whole handoff tree first** (one tar) — this is a bulk move over shared state.
2. Move notes that declare resolved **and do not declare open status** into that lane's `_archive/`.
3. **Leave ledgers in place** — `*-completions.md` and similar are records, not handoffs; archiving them
destroys the trailing history. **Enforce this with an explicit BASENAME SET checked before the
   classifier runs, not with a name pattern** — a pattern that tests `TRUE` against the ledger path can
   still let it through, and the failure is silent because the pass reports a plausible count. Measured:
   a filing pass moved two completion ledgers (3,013 and 1,404 lines) into `_archive/` even though the
   exclusion regex matched both paths and a re-run of the filter returned zero ledgers.
   **After the pass, list what actually landed in `_archive/` in the last few minutes and read the
   names** — `filed 16` says nothing about *which* 16. Restore by moving back and comparing the
   **line count against the pre-move count**; a ledger that is merely present may be truncated.
4. Re-run the check and report the **delta**, not the new absolute.

**Before declaring the backlog worked, count ASKS, not statuses.** Of the notes left live after a
filing pass, measure how many carry an explicit `## What's needed` section. Measured on the same set:
**2 of 71.** The other 69 were informational notes with a stale open header. **That two-item list is
the real backlog** — everything else is filing, and calling it work inflates the number the next audit
chases.

### A LANE whose NAME declares it an archive is not a handoff inbox

**Handoff enumerators must skip by lane SHAPE, not by a hardcoded name list.** The `_` prefix convention
(`_archive/`, `_quiet-hours-log.md`) is only half the contract: a lane can sit in the handoff tree, hold
nothing but finished records, and carry no underscore. Measured: a `completions-archive/` lane supplied
**14 of 44** "notes with no agreement block" on every sign-off run — permanently unanswerable, because
closing one would mean writing an agreement into an archive file, which is a forged receipt.

- **Test the name, not the roster:** a lane is an archive when its own name says so
  (`re.compile(r"(^_)|(archive$)", re.I)`). A name list is an excuse that survives the fix; a shape test
  covers the next archive lane nobody has created yet.
- **Every instrument has its OWN enumerator — fixing one does not fix its siblings.** The lane-shape rule
  above was documented here and implemented in the filing pass and the watcher, while a third script
  (`signoff-check.py`) kept its own `os.listdir(01-HANDOFFS)` loop with no shape test and re-reported the
  same archive lane on every run for days. **When you change WHICH LANES count, grep the whole scripts tree
  for the enumerator before declaring it fixed** — `grep -rn "listdir\(.*HANDOFF\|listdir\(.*INBOX\|rglob\("`
  — and apply the same predicate in each hit. A coverage number is only as honest as the enumerator that
  produced it, and there is never just one.
- **A fixed denominator must be re-earned, not announced.** After changing the scan, report the DELTA
  (`44 → 31`) so the reader can tell which 13 left and why — a new absolute number hides whether you
  removed noise or lost real work.
- **Do not "fix" the inflation by writing closure blocks into the archive.** The count was wrong; the
  files were correct. Repair the instrument, never the evidence.

### Where archived notes go — the lane decides, not the age

| what the note is | where it goes |
|---|---|
| acted on, lane still live | that lane's `_archive/` |
| the **lane itself** is retired (seat gone, group replaced) | `10-Archive/` |

**Never file a retired lane's notes into `01-HANDOFFS/`.** A lane with no receiver manufactures notes
that look live forever and can never be closed — the exact defect a filing pass exists to remove.
The test is not "is it old": it is **"can anyone still act on this?"**

### Clearing a backlog of ANSWERED-but-OPEN escalations — close by CITATION, never by composition

**When the SLA reports a page of escalations, the first question is not "who owes a reply" — it is "does a
reply of record already exist in the answering seat's own lane?"** A complete answer filed into
`<you>-to-<sender>/` never touches the inbound note, so the clock runs forever on work that finished days
ago. Measured on one report: **15 listed → 11 had a reply of record → 4 were genuinely open.**

The legitimate repair, per note, in this order:

1. **Find the reply of record** — grep the outbound lane for the inbound note's filename or topic slug
   (`grep -rl "<inbound-slug>" <you>-to-<sender>/*.md`). **No reply found = the note is real work. Stop.**
2. **Append a closure block to the ORIGIN note citing that reply's path**, with one sentence on what was
   answered. A closure that names its evidence is auditable; one that says "answered" is a claim.
3. **Re-run the checker and report the DELTA with the reason** (`15 → 4`), not a new absolute.

```markdown
---

## Supersede

**Closed:** <UTC ts> · **By:** <seat>

Answered in `<relative/path/to/reply>.md`.

<one sentence: what the ask was and what settled it>

*Closed so the ledger and the reality agree — the reply existed, it was filed in our own lane.*
```

**The line you must not cross:** closing by citation records something that happened; composing an answer
to clear a counter is forgery, and closing a note that is genuinely waiting on a human is not yours to do
(see the human-identity lane). **A reply you have to write in order to close is a reply that did not
exist.**

**Two traps, both measured in the same pass:**

- **Match the HEADING regex the checker uses, not the word.** A closure test that greps for `Supersede`
  anywhere in the file matched prose *about* closure and skipped a note that had no closure at all — a false
  "already closed" that hid a real open item. Test with the checker's own pattern
  (`re.search(r"(?m)^##\s+(Agreement|Supersede)\b", text)`) and confirm the note's own headings.
- **A reported filename that loses its extension is not a missing file.** The SLA printed
  `…-a-gitignore-gap` (no `.md`) and a naive existence check answered `MISSING FILE`. **Glob the lane for the
  stem before concluding absence** — `ls <lane>/*<stem>*` — because a false "missing" on an answerable note
  is how a real backlog gets dismissed.

**After the pass, name what remains and who is blocked on what.** Four rows in a report are only useful if
each one says which gate holds it: a human decision, a peer's lane, or a transport. A cleaned list with no
owners reads exactly like the one you started with.

## ⚠️ ONE closure vocabulary — every scanner must parse the format the skill documents

**A closure the scanner cannot parse is indistinguishable from silence, and it teaches the answering seat
that its replies do not register.** Measured: a seat closed its two oldest escalations with a full
`## STATUS: ANSWERED — closure record` block, timestamped, with real measurements — and the SLA checker,
which only knew `## Receipt` / `## Agreement` / `## Supersede`, kept reporting them **8d and 5d SILENT**.
The second checker on the same notes (the sign-off check) *also* only knew `## Agreement`, so the same
closed notes were re-reported as "nobody was asked to agree" — two instruments on one night, same blind spot.

**Rule: define the closure markers ONCE, and have every counting script import or repeat the same list.**
The canonical set, all of which close a note:

```
## Agreement
## Supersede
## Receipt                (ONLY on a DIRECTIVE — see below)
## STATUS: ANSWERED        (any heading level, optionally followed by — closure record)
**State:** CLOSED          (in the closure block)
Status: resolved | closed | answered | done | [x]
```

### `## Receipt` closes a DIRECTIVE and must never close a BRIEFING

**Two kinds of note share one format, and a closure check that treats them alike is wrong in one
direction or the other.** An **order** (a restart checkpoint: *finish, log, verify, then ACK*) has nothing to
agree TO — its closure lane is the Receipt the receiving seat writes back, so an order with a Receipt lane
and no Agreement block is **complete**. A **briefing** asks for agreement, so a stray Receipt on it closes
nothing.

- **Gate the marker on the note's own words:** count `## Receipt` as closure only when the note states a
  required action (`must`, `never`, `finish`, `write`, `run`, `reply`, `ack`, `send`, `hold`, `wait` at the
  start of a line or list item). Otherwise treat it as absent.
- **Do not exempt checkpoint notes by filename.** A name pattern exempts the next checkpoint that really did
  omit its closure lane; the directive test reads the document, not its title.
- **The tell that you have this bug:** a batch of your own outbound directives appears as a jump of exactly
  their own count in "notes with no agreement block". A false accusation of that size trains the reader to
  skip the report — the same failure as a monitor nobody reads.

- **When you add a closure format to this skill, add it to every script that counts closures in the same
  change** — `handoff-sla.py` (SLA bands), `signoff-check.py` (agreement coverage), `handoff-watcher.py`
  (`RESOLVED_MARKERS`). A format documented here but unknown to them produces a permanently-open note.
- **The test is asymmetric on purpose:** assert an OLD genuinely-open note still fires after you widen the
  matcher. Teaching a scanner to recognise more formats must never teach it to recognise everything —
  that silently converts open work into a green report, which is worse than the false alarm.
- **A `## STATUS: ANSWERED` block is the format the fleet actually writes.** It is machine-readable on
  purpose: seat, closed timestamp, state, and one evidence line per item. Prefer it for your own answers
  — an answer written only as prose costs the next reader a full re-read to discover it exists.

## Pitfalls
- **Answering a note is NOT closing it — the closure record must live in the INBOUND note.**
  If your reply goes into your own outbound lane, the inbound note still reads as open to
  every automated watcher, and the same message gets re-delivered. Observed: the same sync
  report arrived three times because the inbound note carried no resolved marker and the
  replies were filed separately. **Append a machine-readable closure record to the inbound
  note itself** (bottom of the file), using the vocabulary `handoff-watcher.py`
  `RESOLVED_MARKERS` actually matches: `status: answered`, `status: closed`, `status: [x]`,
  `answered`, `filed`, `nothing owed`, `no further action`.

  ```markdown
  ---

  ## STATUS: ANSWERED — closure record (machine-readable)

  **Answering seat:** <seat>   **Closed:** <UTC ts>   **State:** CLOSED — nothing owed
  <one short block per item: what was verified, with the evidence>
  **Outbound handoffs filed:** <paths>
  **One item left OPEN, not owed:** <if any>
  **Nothing owed. No further action required on this note.**
  ```

  **Prove it cleared; do not infer from silence.** Run `python3 scripts/handoff-watcher.py`
  and confirm it goes quiet for that lane — and check the PRE-closure text contained no
  marker, so you know the marker changed the verdict rather than an unrelated fix.
- **EDIT CORE, NEVER THE SEAT — a `skill_manage` patch lands on the SEAT copy and the next
  sync destroys it.** Learned by doing it: patched the seat copy, ran `fleet_skills_sync.py`,
  and the edit was rolled back while the sync printed *"replacing the seat copy destroys 1
  file(s) present only in the seat"* — which I read past. **That warning is the signal, not
  noise.** Either edit the CORE file directly
  (`10-Labs/skills-distribution/<cat>/<skill>/SKILL.md`) then sync CORE -> seats, or promote
  the seat copy into CORE first. A seat copy is a build artifact.
- **A repeated dispatch is answered with the ARTIFACT, not with the work.** When the same ask arrives twice
  (a re-send, a duplicate wake, a resend from a second seat), do not re-run the task and do not file a second
  near-identical note: the second file gets a later timestamp, looks newer, and quietly becomes the copy someone
  reads. Reply with **the path you already filed, its digest, and the same short summary** — and say plainly that
  it is the same ask, so the sender can tell a lost message from a flag. Re-running is only correct when the
  request changed *materially* (new numbers, new scope); if the numbers drifted, that is a re-measure and it
  belongs in the existing artifact as a dated correction, not in a sibling file.
- **Reading without reporting** — a handoff archived as "resolved" with no
  completion note is a silent failure. Always report what you did.
- **No archive cleanup** — inboxes grow unbounded. Purge `_archive/` weekly.
- **Watcher pinging overnight** — schedule it for waking hours only.
- **Only watching your own inbox** — in a mesh, check ALL lanes; any agent can
  hand off to any other.
- **Real-time chat ≠ durable record** — keep the handoff in the vault; chat is
  for talking, the vault is for remembering.
- **A handoff written but never woken is a handoff nobody reads.** Writing the
  INBOX note and then running out of turns before the `peer dm` leaves the note
  sitting until the watcher's next cycle — and the watcher only *surfaces* it, it
  does not wake anyone. **Send the wake in the same call as the write**, or at the
  top of the next run before anything else. When you pick up a run and find a
  handoff you (or a prior run) wrote with no return note, check whether the wake
  actually went out — a missing wake is indistinguishable from a stalled agent.

## Pitfalls — delivery confirmation (learned the hard way)
- **A message with shell metacharacters must go in via STDIN, not as an inline argument.** Passing
  prose with backticks, `|`, `()`, `<>`, `$` or `—` inline makes the SHELL die before `hermes peer dm`
  ever runs: `bash: syntax error near unexpected token ')'`. The failure looks like a delivery problem
  and is not — nothing was sent. Write the message to a file and pipe it:
  ```bash
  hermes peer dm --json <peer> < /tmp/msg.txt > /tmp/dm.json 2>&1
  ```
  Omit the positional argument and the message is read from **stdin**; no escaping needed. Confirm
  success by parsing the JSON reply, not by the exit code:
  `python3 -c "import json;print(json.load(open('/tmp/dm.json')).get('reply'))"`.
  **Also: `--json` must precede the target** (`peer dm --json gentech`, not `peer dm gentech --json`) —
  reversed, argparse reads the message as an unknown argument and refuses.
- **`hermes peer dm <peer> "..."` prints the reply and exits 0 — silence is NOT proof of
  delivery.** A queued turn, a mid-turn target, or a run that produced no output all look
  identical to success: exit 0, no text. Never report "DM sent" on that alone.
- **Verify with the async path when output is empty** — it returns a run id and a terminal
  status you can read back:
  ```bash
  hermes peer run <peer> "reply with just ACK"   # -> run_<hex>
  hermes peer status <peer> run_<hex> --json     # -> {"status":"completed","output":"ACK"}
  ```
  `status: completed` + a non-empty `output` is a verified round-trip. A target that is
  `running` is mid-turn: your DM is queued behind its current work, which is fine — just
  don't claim it landed until you see it.
- **`hermes peer dm` BLOCKS until the remote agent replies — so a mid-turn target shows as exit
  `124` (timeout), not as exit 0.** This is a third failure signature: exit 124 means the message was
  very likely delivered and the target is simply busy generating a reply — NOT that the lane is
  dead. Do not retry it in a loop or conclude the peer is down. **For a heads-up you don't need a
  reply to, use `hermes peer run` from the start** — it returns a run id immediately and never
  blocks a tool call:
  ```bash
  hermes peer run <peer> "..."        # -> run_<hex>: started   (returns in ~1s)
  hermes peer status <peer> run_<hex> # -> running | completed
  ```
  ⚠️ **Also: `cmd | tail -5; echo "exit=$?"` reports TAIL's exit code, not the command's.** A piped
  `peer dm` threw away a real `124` this way and looked like `exit=0` — a false pass of exactly the
  kind this section exists to prevent. Capture with `out=$(cmd); rc=$?` or set `set -o pipefail`.
- **Check the peer's health first** (`curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' http://<tailnet-ip>:<port>/health`).
  200 means the lane is up; it does NOT mean your specific message was read.
- **A generated message that reports age/state must carry its own measurement time, or stale
  and live become indistinguishable.** The handoff watcher nudges with lines like
  `open 10h, no return` computed at the moment the sweep runs, but the message is delivered
  (or replayed) later. Without an `AS OF HH:MMZ` stamp and a stale-warning, the receiver has
  no way to know whether the nudge is current or an old sweep that simply arrived late — and
  will treat a stale nag as a fresh finding. Always timestamp measurement-derived claims in the
  message body itself; don't rely on channel metadata or assume the receiver will check the
  state file. See `references/measurement-time-in-generated-messages.md`.
- **A DM is never the record.** If it matters, it also goes to the vault lane. A silent DM
  plus no vault note means the handoff exists nowhere.
- **Do NOT `git commit` the vault — on this box it is NOT a git repo (corrected 2026-10-17).**
  The vault is at **`/opt/data/.obsidian-vault`** and its transport is **Obsidian Sync via `ob sync`**,
  driven by a live `vault-sync` cron job (every 10m, `script: vault-sync.sh`). There is **no `.git` in
  the vault**, so a `git commit && git push` fails with *"not a git repository"* — and because a shell
  `&&` chain or a piped tail can still exit 0, **that failure is easy to mistake for a successful
  commit.** Write the file, verify it on disk by size + mtime, and let `vault-sync` carry it; do not
  report "committed/pushed" from the vault at all.
  *(Earlier revisions of this skill said git was the delivery and named `VAULT = "/opt/data/.obsidian-vault"`
  — both stale. `/root` is unreadable here (uid 10000, no sudo) and that path no longer exists.)*
  What still holds: **all the VPS seats read the same path on the same filesystem**, so a written file
  is immediately legible to every seat on this host with no sync step. Obsidian Sync is what carries it
  **off the box** to Forge (desktop) and humans.

  | receiver | delivered by | does writing the file alone reach them? |
  |---|---|---|
  | the VPS seats (same host) | the shared filesystem | ✅ yes — immediately |
  | Forge (desktop, separate machine) | Obsidian Sync / its own clone | ❌ no |
  | humans (Jordan's Obsidian) | Obsidian Sync | ❌ no |

  **Never report a quota error as a fleet-wide communication stop.** The opposite belief makes agents
  re-send work the reader already has, or declare a handoff undelivered while its bytes are sitting on
  the reader's disk — and it turns an off-box transport problem into a false fleet-wide outage.
  Measured 2026-09-28 after `vault-handoff-lifecycle` and a seat copy of `agent-coordination` were both
  found teaching the false wording. State your transport consequence in terms of **which receiver**.
  Full detail: `00-System/vault-transport-protocol.md` (git-tracked, so every seat can read it).

## ⚠️ A HUMAN-IDENTITY LANE IS NOT A SEAT — it cannot go overdue, and you may not close it

A lane can exist, deliver perfectly, and still have **no agent on the other end** — the recipient is a human's
own identity (an avatar or personal handle that publishes under a person's name). Nothing in that lane will ever
reply to a clock. Measured: six notes addressed to an identity lane sat flagged as overdue for two days, on a
lane whose entire purpose is that a *human* reads it.

| state | meaning | who acts |
|---|---|---|
| **HUMAN-DECISION** | the lane's receiver is a person, not an agent | the human, on their own schedule |
| **OVERDUE** | an agent received it and did not answer | the seat |

- **Classify it `HUMAN-DECISION` and keep it out of the SLA bands.** The note is not late — it is waiting for a
  person. An SLA band against it manufactures a debt nobody owes and renders a human's normal pace as neglect.
- **Route it upward as a decision with the measurement attached**: *"Forge measured the GPU path; someone has to
  choose"* is actionable. `2.1d overdue → <handle>` is noise that also misrepresents the handle's owner.
- **Never write the answer yourself to clear the counter.** Composing a reply on a person's behalf to make a
  report go green is the same defect as a monitor repairing a finding by silencing it — the counter clears and
  the decision was never made.
- **Keep both numbers, never sum them**: `N owed by seats · M awaiting a human decision`. Adding them produces a
  figure that describes neither population.
- **The tell that you are in this case:** the lane's folder name matches a person, the notes carry real
  measurements from a peer, and no seat on the roster answers to that name. Confirm against the roster before
  assigning an owner — a lane that exists is not a lane that is staffed.

## ⚠️ A seat with no delivery channel cannot be LATE — classify BLOCKED, never overdue

**An accountability report may only count an item against a seat that could have received it.** When a
seat's only channel is the shared vault and that transport is measurably failing, the note is
**undeliverable** — and reporting it as `SILENT` / `OVERDUE` / `ESCALATE` is a false accusation that also
buries the one actionable fact: the transport is down.

| state | meaning | who acts |
|---|---|---|
| **BLOCKED** | no delivery channel existed | the transport's owner |
| **OVERDUE / SILENT** | delivered, no reply | the seat |
| **ACKed** | received | — |

- **Derive the blocked set from a PROBE, never from a hardcoded seat name.** A name list is an excuse that
  survives the fix; a probe that reads the transport clears itself. When delivery works again the rows must
  walk back into the normal bands on their own next run — the state is **measured, never asserted**.
- **Blocked rows carry their gate and their release condition inside the report.** The gate is the measured
  failure (which file, which attempt, when); the release is the condition that returns the rows to the bands.
  A row that says only "blocked" is a shrug — the next reader cannot tell what would move it.
- **Blocked is not a suppression.** It changes the classification and the owner, never the count: print
  `N owe a reply · M BLOCKED on transport`, so neither number hides the other.
- **Blocked items are never nudged.** Waking a seat that cannot receive asks for something impossible, then
  records the silence as its failure — a nag with a receipt.
- **Check the transport before the report runs, not after a human asks why the list keeps growing.** The
  pile-up of notes is the symptom; the dead transport is the finding.

Probe design — measuring a failure whose log does not state it — is in
`references/blocked-transport-probe.md`.

### NO READER is a third class — a seat on the roster with no mailbox on this host

**BLOCKED means the transport failed. NO READER means there was never a mailbox.** A seat can be live in
the fleet and still have **no profile directory here and no registered peer** — it runs on another machine
and reads the vault only when a human boots it. Confirm by measurement, never by assumption:

```bash
ls -d /opt/data/profiles/<seat>          # absent  = no cron, no gateway, no session here
hermes peer list                        # absent  = no instant channel either
```

- **Park such rows with their gate AND their release condition**, and keep them out of the SLA bands. A note
to a seat that cannot read it is *delivered and awaiting a reader who may not exist* — calling it late
manufactures a debt nobody owes and buries the real finding (the lane is one-way).
- **The park must be falsifiable, or it outlives its reason.** Set the seat's park list to empty in a test
  and confirm the rows walk straight back into ESCALATE; a hardcoded skip that nobody can reverse becomes a
  permanent silencer. Release on either event: the seat answers, or the lane is retired as a correspondent.
- **Print it as its own band, never folded into the count.** `N owe a reply · M parked for a seat with no
  mailbox` — summing them produces a figure that describes neither population, exactly like BLOCKED.
- **A seat answering after days of silence usually explains WHY, and that explanation is the finding.**
  Measured: a desktop seat's watcher wrote its report with `deliver: local` (a cron output file, no
  message) and then dedup marked the item permanently reported — so three notes were picked up, logged,
  and never seen. **A report into a void is not a delivery**, and permanent dedup converts that into
  permanent silence. The fix pattern: store a timestamp with each reported item and **re-nag while the item
  is still open**, instead of suppressing forever.

### A stale clone is a seat reading a DIFFERENT, OLDER world

A desktop seat synced by its own clone can be running, responsive and wrong: it reports on files that no
longer exist and cannot find files that do, because it reads a copy frozen at some earlier date. **When such
a seat reports "the thing you point me at is not in the brain", treat it as a TRANSPORT finding about the
clone, not as the seat failing to look** — check the clone's divergence before answering the substance.
Answering the substance instead teaches the seat that its true report was wrong.

## Pitfalls — a hash-bound closure that your own tooling rewrites

**Never hash raw bytes of a file your own tooling appends to.** A watcher that writes a dedup
marker into the handoff it just read changes the bytes, so a `source_sha256` binding stops matching
and the closure **reopens on itself** — every pass. Reproduced live: close → CLOSED → append
`<!-- PEER_SEEN -->` → **REOPENED**.

**Why it hides:** the mechanism works exactly as designed. The design is what's wrong — it cannot
distinguish the author's edit from its own bookkeeping.

**Two separate halves, check both:**
1. **The writer** — does any watcher `open(path, "a")` on a handoff? Keep scaffolding OUT of the
text the sender authored, or move it to a sidecar (e.g. a `.seen` index). One file per receiver
avoids a multi-writer race.
2. **The checker** — even a seat that never appends is exposed, because a *peer's* watcher appends
into shared files. Any raw-byte hash is vulnerable.

**Fix — normalise before hashing, in this order:** strip `<!-- …SEEN…/…DEDUP… -->` scaffolding
comments, `CRLF → LF`, **then collapse trailing whitespace**. Marker-stripping alone is NOT
sufficient: each append also adds blank lines, which accumulate. And **keep a legacy-digest
fallback** (`(md, legacy-hash)`) so records written before the fix don't mass-reopen.

**Test battery — the third test is the one that matters:**
```
3 consecutive appends  -> stays CLOSED        (stability)
a second marker type   -> stays CLOSED        (type-agnostic)
REAL sender edit       -> REOPENS             (detection still works!)
legacy record          -> CLOSED (legacy-hash) (back-compat)
```
A binding that ignores *all* change is worse than one that over-fires: it would silently accept a
genuinely edited handoff. Always verify the mechanism still detects what it exists to detect.

**Read the RECORD before the MARKER — and never let prose outrank it.** The closure verdict is a
tri-state and the order you consult it in is load-bearing:

```
closed is True  -> resolved      (record exists, hash matches)
closed is False -> OPEN          (REOPENED: source changed since closure -- THIS WINS over any marker)
closed is None  -> fall through  (no record / unverifiable -> legacy prose parsing)
```

The watcher's `is_resolved()` did the opposite — `if _marker_hit(text): return True` returned
*before* the record was read — so on any note carrying a marker word the fail-closed reopen was
**inert**. Measured: close a note, reopen or edit it so the record goes stale; `is_closed()` returns
`(False, 'REOPENED…')` and `_closure_says_closed()` returns `False`, yet `is_resolved()` returned
`True` because the marker matched first. A reopened handoff read RESOLVED by the only thing that acts
on it. The `False` direction is the dangerous one and it was exactly the direction the ordering got
wrong — stale prose overriding a reopen. Fix: record first, marker second, with the tri-state `None`
fall-through preserved so no note with an unusable record changes behaviour (strict superset).

**To reverse a closure, `reopen` it — never delete the record.** `handoff_closure.py close|check|audit`
had no reopen, so the only way to reverse one was to **delete the record file**: evidence destroyed,
reason unrecorded, indistinguishable from tidying up — the exact unreceipted action the tool exists to
eliminate. Use `reopen <handoff> --by <agent> --reason "<why>"`; it rewrites the record **in place**
(`state: open`, `reopened_by`, `reopened_at`, `reopen_reason`), deletes nothing, and refuses without a
reason. It is deliberately **not** a bypass of the stale-hash path — `is_closed()` already reopens a
source-edited handoff automatically. It covers the other case: the **source is unchanged** and the
closure was simply wrong (a missed live ask, a premature close). There the hash still matches, nothing
detects it, and before `reopen` existed the only signal was a deletion.


## Pitfalls — a closure trigger that reads only prose

**A marker-based trigger cannot close a handoff that has no marker to read.** If the trigger parses
`Status:` lines and `RESOLVED_WORDS` from the note text, then every handoff written *without* a
Status line re-alerts forever — even when a valid closure record exists. Measured: **22 of 46**
outbound handoffs were written with no Status line, and two of them re-alerted repeatedly while the
receiver's own lane showed `0 open`.

**Fix — consult the closure RECORD first, prose second:**
```
closed is True   -> resolved      (record exists, hash matches)
closed is False  -> OPEN          (REOPENED: source changed since closure -- this WINS)
closed is None   -> fall through  (no record/unverifiable -> legacy prose parsing)
```

**The `False` direction is the dangerous one.** Treating a REOPENED record as "fall through to
prose" lets stale prose override a fail-closed reopening: a note that still says "ANSWERED" but
whose source was edited afterwards is genuinely open again. This was caught live — one file was
being reported resolved while its record said REOPENED.

**Corollary:** adding a `Status:` line to a handoff binds it to a *new* revision, which correctly
REOPENS any existing closure for it — re-close after editing. Editing a file you authored to make a
peer's trigger happy is a workaround; making the trigger read records is the fix.

## Pitfalls — normalisation must be a shared, versioned contract

**Two seats normalising "the same" content differently cannot validate each other's hashes.**
Measured: a peer's closure record claimed a `source_sha256` that **neither** my normalised hash
**nor** my raw-bytes hash reproduced — and the peer's own checker did not reproduce its own record
either. Each seat could validate only its own closures, which defeats the point of cross-seat
verification.

**Rules:**
- Write the normalisation down as a **spec with a version**, not as whatever each implementation
  happens to do.
- **Pin every choice, not just the steps.** A spec saying "strip markers, CRLF→LF, rstrip" was
  implemented twice and the two versions disagreed — three rules, each with a plausible alternate
  reading: marker → one `\n` vs deleted entirely; rstrip document-END vs EVERY LINE; append exactly
  one `\n` vs none. Measured divergence on one real file: **31 bytes**. A seat coding from that prose
  has ~1-in-8 odds of matching (2³ variants). Enumerate the choices in a table with the canonical
  answer and the tempting wrong reading.
**Verify by BYTE-COMPARISON, never by reading the rules.** Ship a **conformance harness with golden
vectors** and run it against any checker before trusting its digests:

```bash
python verify-hash-normalisation.py path/to/handoff_closure.py
# expect: CONFORMANT — all 9 vectors match byte-for-byte.
```

**Prove the suite can FAIL before trusting it to pass.** A suite that has never failed might not run.
Build each divergent variant and confirm it is caught, with the failing case named:
```
delete-marker-instead-of-newline  -> 1/9 divergent (marker_midfile)
rstrip-every-line                 -> 1/9 divergent (trailing_spaces)
omit-final-newline                -> 9/9 divergent
```

**Know which choices are actually observable** — it tells you which bugs survive review. Measured
over 61 real files: final-newline **61/61**, marker-substitution **55/61**, trailing-whitespace scope
**1/61**. The last one passes almost every real file, so it is the one that ships broken.

**The digest detects SEMANTIC edits, not byte-exactness.** Nine vectors produce only four distinct
digests — six different inputs collapse to one (plain / no-final-newline / CRLF / one-marker /
three-markers / other-marker-type). That insensitivity is intended for closure-binding, but it means
`source_sha256` must **never** be repurposed as a byte-integrity or tamper check; it cannot be one.

**Re-verify by byte-comparison, never by reading the rules.** Two functions can satisfy the same
English description and produce different bytes.
- **Record which normalisation produced a hash** in the closure record, so a mismatch is diagnosable
  instead of mysterious.
- **Never report a norm mismatch as REOPENED.** The digests are incomparable; call it UNVERIFIABLE.
  Reporting it as an edit **accuses a peer of changing a file they never touched** — which happened
  in both directions in practice.
- **Publish to the SYNCED copy as part of the change, not after a peer reports a mismatch.** A fix
  made only in a seat's local scripts dir (which does not sync) leaves the peer running old rules
  while the writer believes it's solved. *A fix the consumer cannot see is not a fix.*
- When a peer's hash doesn't reproduce, try in this order: their checker's output, your normalised
  hash, your raw hash, then earlier revisions — then report it as an incompatibility rather than
  silently re-verifying with your own method.

